<!-- ACCESS MODIFIERS -->
<section class="oop-section">
    <div class="oop-section-header">
        <div class="oop-section-icon">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-lock"></i>
        </div>
        <h2 class="oop-section-title">Access Modifiers in Python (Concept)</h2>
    </div>
    
    <div class="keywords-table">
        <table>
            <thead>
                <tr>
                    <th style="width: 30%;">Visibility</th>
                    <th>Python Convention</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td><span class="keyword-term">Public</span></td>
                    <td>No underscore, open to use anywhere</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><span class="keyword-term">Protected</span></td>
                    <td>Single underscore (example: _items). A convention: "use inside the class or subclass."</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><span class="keyword-term">Private-ish</span></td>
                    <td>Double underscore (example: __items). Name-mangling discourages outside use.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    
    <p class="section-intro" style="margin-top: 1rem;">
        Python does not enforce access modifiers like Java or C#. It uses naming conventions to support
        encapsulation. In this project, inventory items are stored in a protected list to encourage
        safe access through methods.
    </p>
</section>

